% ======================================================================================== 4 Methodology
\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}
\para{The fast tier and v2} We flag a score move when the 10\,s volume-weighted price moves at least
<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢ away from its prior 60\,s. Each month a wallet joins the fast tier if, in earlier months, it
traded at least << V('pol.q_prints') >> times 0--3\,s after a flag, in at least << V('pol.q_matches') >>
matches, with \(t > << V('pol.q_t') >>\). v2 copies their trades, sizes each by its resolution risk (the best of
<< V('sel.sizing.n') >> sizing rules, picked walk-forward), caps orders at << V('risk.order_usd') >> and exposure at
<< V('risk.netcap') >> shares net per match, and holds to resolution. At their own prices it measures what their speed
is worth, not what we could earn: copying the same trades 3\,s later loses (Fig.~\ref{fig:edge}a).

\para{Being fast ourselves} Our second strategy: a computer-vision (CV) model watches the video, calls the point
early and sends a taker order before the stale quote is cancelled. We have no video of the matches we trade,
so we simulate this trader on past points with an assumed feed latency (licensed feed not purchased); all other
parameters are measured. The order wins only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
  V + \delta + D - \ell \;<\; L + R \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad V \;<\; L + R - D - \delta + \ell ,
\end{equation}
where \(V\) is the feed delay (how late the video reaches us), \(\delta\) our pipeline plus network time, \(D\) the
<< V('venue.delay') >>\,s taker hold, \(\ell\) how early the CV calls the point, \(L\) the \emph{stamp lag} (how long
after the ball lands the official point stamp is logged) and \(R\) when the book reprices relative to that
stamp. On << V('lat.n_points') >> points the book moved << V('lat.book_vs_stamp') >>\,s before the stamp. Nobody
publishes \(L\), so we show two readings: << V('cv.pre.lag') >>\,s, which we \emph{pre-registered} (written before any
result; \texttt{PREREG.md}, << V('t0.prereg.written') >> UTC), and << V('cv.cal.lag') >>\,s, a \emph{post
hoc} estimate from one day of data. The simulated trades are past points where the price later moved \(\geq\)<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢:
\emph{selected on outcomes, not ex ante}.

\para{Our vision on real video} On the TrackNet broadcast set we built court calibration, a 3D flight fit and landing
predictors, and tested them once on games 8--10 (<< V('bt.bounces.test') >> bounces). From one TV camera, on the
pretrained detector's own track, the landing point is << V('bt.err0') >>\,cm off at the bounce and
<< V('bt.err.lead') >>\,m off when predicted 33--300\,ms ahead (median). With thresholds set on games 1--7, out calls
were right << V('bt.call.0') >> at the bounce (half the outs), << V('bt.call.33') >> at 33\,ms and
<< V('bt.call.67') >> at 67\,ms ahead, and fail from 100\,ms (<< V('bt.call.100') >>). Only the bounce-time call met our
95\% precision rule on games 1--7, and the detector weights \citep{yastrebksv}, not ours, saw frames of all ten games,
so these are in-distribution numbers. On simulated Hawk-Eye-class physics our spin-aware model cuts the landing
spread << V('spin.ratio200') >>-fold at 200\,ms (out-call precision << V('spin.out.prec200') >>, recall
<< V('spin.out.rec200') >>). On held-out table tennis at 120\,fps our live causal engine called
<< V('cv.eng.tp') >> of << V('cv.eng.nmiss') >> misses early (median lead << V('cv.eng.lead') >>\,ms), none wrongly, at
<< V('cv.eng.fps') >>\,fps on one L4 GPU; outside play it makes about << V('cv.phantom.hr') >> phantom calls an hour,
and a rally gate (trade a miss call only within << V('gate.s') >>\,s of a bounce call, set before the test) removes
<< V('gate.out.removed') >> of << V('gate.out.total') >> calls on unlabelled balls in its held-out log but keeps only
<< V('gate.correct.kept') >> of << V('gate.correct.total') >> correct calls and leaves about << V('gate.hr') >> an hour:
not yet safe to trade. With a simulated 1\,s feed, our << V('e2e.ours') >>\,ms pipeline,
<< V('e2e.net') >>\,ms of network and the 1\,s hold, an order can execute << V('e2e.total') >>\,ms after the point
(p99 << V('e2e.p99') >>\,ms; << V('e2e.n') >> runs, order built, not sent), inside the organizers' 3\,s bar
(Fig.~\ref{fig:f2t}).

\para{Costs and validation} Takers pay \(f(q) = r\,q\,(1-q)\) a share at price \(q\), or \(10{,}000\,r\,(1-q)\) bps of
notional; today \(r = << V('fee.rate') >>\), so a trade at 50¢ pays << V('calc.fee.q50.c') >>¢. Each match pays its own
fee and every trade the spread it crossed; we also double both. v2 paid << V('v2.is.fee_bps') >> and
<< V('v2.oos.fee_bps') >>\,bps in fees (IS, OOS) and still netted << V('v2.is.net_bps') >> and
<< V('v2.oos.net_bps') >>\,bps. We tune only in sample, walk-forward (fit on past months, test on the next).

