\section{Liquidity and capacity}\label{sec:liq}
\para{How much capital} v2: << V('cap.1x.oos.capital') >>, the largest size (1\(\times\)) at which its OOS CI stays above
zero (5\(\times\) loses); it trades << V('v2.is.notional_day') >> a day, << V('v2.is.share_vol') >> of match volume. The
CV book: << V('capcv.half.oos') >>--<< V('capcv.half.is') >> post hoc (OOS--IS), where its Sharpe halves, and at most
<< V('capcv.pmax.oos.day') >> a day (on << V('capcv.pmax.oos') >> OOS; Fig.~\ref{fig:capacity}); pre-registered, none
(Sharpe << V('capcv.pre.sr0.is') >> IS, << V('capcv.pre.sr0.oos') >> OOS at its smallest size). It takes at most half the
stale depth at each point: << V('capcv.share_inplay') >> of in-play volume but << V('capcv.share_fast') >> of what the
fast tier trades 0--3\,s after a point. These are upper bounds: we model no price impact beyond the stale depth, and up
to << V('ft.wallets.last') >> fast wallets compete for the same quotes. At 1\,s the book could pay at most
<< V('cv.cal.oos.maxlic') >> a month for data post hoc and << V('cv.pre.oos.maxlic') >> pre-registered, against feed
prices of << V('fin.feed.low') >>--<< V('fin.feed.high') >>. At today's fee COURTSIDE prices speed; it is not yet a
business.

% ======================================================================================== 8 Limitations
\section{Limitations and next steps}\label{sec:limits}
The stamp lag \(L\) is unmeasured and moves the break-even one for one (Eq.~\ref{eq:budget}); the 1\,s hold dominates
it, since even for a 20\,ms bot the inferred lag is << V('cv.L_bot') >>\,s. One session with a licensed feed and a few
test orders would measure \(L\); at 1\,s the feed pays for itself only if \(L \geq << V('cv.L_go') >>\)\,s, a purchase
we have not made. Our out calls are tested on real broadcast tennis and table tennis, never on
the matches we trade; trading is simulated and we never sent an order. A fee rise, a shorter hold or more fast wallets
would break it.\label{lastmain}

